															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-reach" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1779895); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1779895&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349732" alt="Dr Reach" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1779895)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dr-reach" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1779895); return false;" >Dr Reach</a></span> <span class="odate time_1417442145 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Dec 2014 13:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2169790">
									

<p>I want to upvote this for a number of reasons. Mostly the concept, which I find amazingly interesting and even played with in the &quot;Centre for Escathological and Transcendental Research&quot;, a GOI contest detail related to MCF; the idea that, yes, there are souls (consciences, identities, &quot;pilots&quot;, name what you will), but they are not what you may think, is cool.</p>
<p>And then the narrative sort of went into explaining how this was possible, and what did it imply&#8230; which sort of killed it for me. Would microwormholes even be researched by memeticists? Shouldn't physicists be researching this, taking the legacy left by neurologists? It's a very nice attempt at doing science - exposition, but I became distraught. The ending was sort of underwhelming and distraught, which is another problem, and not one I care about particularly.</p>
<p>Finally, this is not exactly a dystopia, not for my definition of it anyways. It's attractive, interesting and filled with truly interesting implications that I'd love to see expanded upon (as Kalinin pointed out in his comment upthread), but the tale simply dumps them at the reader, then doesn't go any further. There is no evident suffering, there is just a soul surgeon (the Foundation) doing careful soul surgery (Stage Fright) on all of humanity; the O5s are merely biding their time, and it is a piss poor attempt at it. There is no societal decay, no environmental decay, and there is basically no politics mentioned asides from what would be required to explain how little the world beyond the Foundation actually changed&#8230; which, of course, is par for the course. How would anyone notice that souls, as depicted in this setting, are gone? They seem to be superfluous. This angers me a bit. I wanted more of that, and did not see it.</p>
<p>And still&#8230;</p>
<p>Still, I love this tale (and everything memetics-related) and I will upvote after the contest is over. Good work. :)</p>

							</div>

								
					
